@attribute [Post("Reviving the $20 Xbox", 2026, 10, 21)]
@attribute [Tags("gaming", "modding", "repair", "3d-printing")]
@attribute [Slug("twenty-dollar-xbox")]
@attribute [OpenGraph("Getting a $20 broken Xbox to a usable state with some mistakes along the way", "/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4878.HEIC", "Inside of modified PowerMac G5 chassis")]

<p>
    I picked up a broken Xbox for parts to repair my trusty old one and ended up not needing anything from it. This
    little fella was pretty crosty. Reeked of cigarette smoke and pretty dirty.
</p>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4466.HEIC"
         alt="Close-up picture of Xbox 1.0 motherboard with leaked capacitor juices and corrosion.">
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4527.HEIC"
         alt="The motherboard immersed in an ultrasonic cleaner.">
</gallery>

<p>
    There was some gnarly corrosion around the clock capacitor - we jumped straight to drastic measures and tossed it in
    the ultrasonic bath with some vinegar then dried it out in my toaster oven (only at like 150&deg;F). It was really
    satisfying seeing all the corrosion just dissolve off the board in seconds.
</p>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/vlcsnap-2026-10-21-21h59m15s854.png"
         alt="Xbox boot error showing a code 11.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/vlcsnap-2026-10-21-21h59m34s761.png"
         alt="&quot;Christmas Lights&amp; around the power button of the failing Xbox.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4705.HEIC"
         alt="Board from the failed DVD drive.">
</gallery>

<p>
    The Xbox booted into the service screen with error code 11, indicating a faulty optical drive. I was able to confirm
    that it worked fine with the DVD drive from my other Xbox so that made narrowing it down pretty easy. Replacing the
    capacitors in the drive's logic board didn't remedy the situation and replacement drives are outlandishly expensive,
    so sadly I relegated this Xbox to a digital-only existence.
</p>

<p>
    The original Xbox won't boot without a somewhat-functioning DVD drive connected, meaning I would need to do my first
    modchip install to make it usable. I ordered a cheap Modxo unit from AliExpress and waited a couple of weeks.
</p>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4708.HEIC"
         alt="Close-up of wire soldered to D0 pin on motherboard.">
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4706.HEIC"
         alt="Xbox with improperly installed modchip.">
</gallery>

<p>
    Did some soldering... aaaand I plugged the modchip in backwards. That fried it. Back to AliExpress and another
    3-week wait because my local USPS hub was going through some renovations.
</p>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/vlcsnap-2026-10-21-22h14m22s341.png"
         alt="Xbox booted into PrometheOS.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/vlcsnap-2026-10-21-22h14m31s058.png"
         alt="Xbox showing Cerbios boot error.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/vlcsnap-2026-10-21-22h14m53s703.png"
         alt="Xbox successfully booting Cerbios.">
</gallery>

<p>
    Several weeks later, I plugged the replacement modchip in the correct way. I didn't want to put a dead DVD drive
    back in here and thought maybe it'd be cool to see if there were any driveless enclosures people had designed to 3D
    print. There was a really cool one I liked on Etsy but it cost a pretty penny. Then I came across the devilishly
    simple "slimming" process that people have been doing to these consoles since time immemorial. This just struck me
    as such a goofy thing to do and really felt like it fit the early 2000's modded console aesthetic that I think of
    with early Xboxes so I decided to try it out.
</p>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4871.HEIC" alt="Trimmed front panel.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4872.HEIC" alt="Trimmed top panel.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4873.HEIC" alt="Trimmed top panel, alternate angle.">

    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4868.HEIC" alt="Trimmed Faraday cage.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4869.HEIC" alt="Trimmed Faraday cage, angle 2.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4870.HEIC" alt="Trimmed Faraday cage, angle 3.">
</gallery>
<p>
    The shape of the metal shielding in this console really reminds me of the silhouette of the One X and One S
    consoles, fun little detail.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4874.HEIC" alt="Trimmed bottom panel.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4875.HEIC" alt="Trimmed bottom panel, alternate angle.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4876.HEIC" alt="Bottom panel with Faraday cage.">
</gallery>
<p>
    I used my Ridgid 3" cut-off tool rather than a dremel to do this and it made very quick work of it. The cutting disc
    was still thin enough and the increased size made it a lot easier to follow a straight line than using a little
    Dremel to make the cuts.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4878.HEIC" alt="Test fit assembly.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4879.HEIC" alt="Test fit assembly, angle 2.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4880.HEIC" alt="Test fit assembly, angle 3.">
</gallery>
<p>
    I haven't even filed or sanded anything and it looks pretty decent put together. Pretty happy with that, actually.
    <br/>
    I still need to put in a smaller fan that actually fits and make a mounting bracket for the 2.5" hard disk I'm
    using.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="2/5" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4885.HEIC" alt="Disk bracket in Xbox.">
    <img scaled basis="3/5" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4886.HEIC" alt="Disk bracket with drive installed.">
    <img scaled basis="3/5" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/screenshot.png" alt="Screenshot of design in Autodesk Fusion.">
</gallery>
<p>
    Took a few rounds of revisions to clear all the board components but I got the HDD snugly tucked between the modchip
    and GPU heatsink.
</p>
<external-link site="ExternalSite.Printables" value="1886962-25-drive-bracket-for-original-xbox-slim-mod"
               label="2.5″ Drive Bracket"></external-link>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4893.HEIC" alt="Fan with bracket installed.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4894.HEIC" alt="Fan with bracket installed, alternate angle.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/bracket-screenshot.png"
         alt="View of 3D model in Autodesk Fusion.">
</gallery>
<p>
    I made a slimmer version of a fan mount I made on Printables to increase vertical clearance a little with a Noctua
    60mm fan.
</p>
<external-link site="ExternalSite.Printables" value="1887498-slim-xbox-60mm-fan-mount"
               label="Exhaust Fan Mount"></external-link>

<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4897.HEIC" alt="Fan with duct installed.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/IMG_4898.HEIC" alt="Fan with duct installed, alternate angle.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-10-Xbox/duct-screenshot.png"
         alt="View of 3D model in Autodesk Fusion.">
</gallery>
<p>
    I also made a little duct to pull some more air through the CPU's heatsink. I tried not to go too far with it since
    there are other passive components on this board that will need cooling.
</p>

<external-link site="ExternalSite.Printables" value="1887501-xbox-10-60mm-fan-duct"
               label="CPU Heatsink Shroud"></external-link>

<p>
    And that's everything buttoned up!  This ended up being a very fun project indeed!
</p>

